00:00:06.600 --> 00:00:09.490
link in the description.

00:00:09.490 --> 00:00:11.800
Imagine an patented apple.

00:00:11.800 --> 00:00:14.780
Just like only Nabisco can sell Oreos and
only Unilever

00:00:14.780 --> 00:00:20.470
can sell Marmite, imagine there was only one
company worldwide with the right to harvest

00:00:20.470 --> 00:00:21.520
apples.

00:00:21.520 --> 00:00:23.840
That would be ridiculous, wouldn’t it?

00:00:23.840 --> 00:00:27.100
Now imagine a patented gene sequence.

00:00:27.100 --> 00:00:32.760
Imagine a single company had exclusive rights
to all usages of a particular section of the

00:00:32.760 --> 00:00:34.210
human genome.

00:00:34.210 --> 00:00:37.269
That would also be absurd, right?

00:00:37.269 --> 00:00:42.479
Back in 1990, renowned geneticist Mary-Claire
King made an announcement that shook

00:00:42.479 --> 00:00:47.429
the world—she and her research team had
discovered that somewhere in this section

00:00:47.429 --> 00:00:50.899
of the
17th chromosome was a gene linked to breast

00:00:50.899 --> 00:00:51.899
cancer.

00:00:51.899 --> 00:00:57.030
She didn’t know exactly where it was,
or what it was, or why it was linked to cancer,

00:00:57.030 --> 00:00:59.190
all she knew was that it was there.

00:00:59.190 --> 00:01:01.899
What
followed was a race to find this so-called

00:01:01.899 --> 00:01:03.229
“breast-cancer gene.”

00:01:03.229 --> 00:01:06.840
Colleges, companies, and
governments all devoted enormous resources

00:01:06.840 --> 00:01:09.539
to finding the gene that could tell people,
with

00:01:09.539 --> 00:01:14.689
relatively accuracy, whether they would contract
cancer years before any other test.

00:01:14.689 --> 00:01:17.250
With
preventative mastectomies, survival rates

00:01:17.250 --> 00:01:19.290
for this type of cancer could skyrocket.

00:01:19.290 --> 00:01:22.619
Then, in 1994,
it happened.

00:01:22.619 --> 00:01:26.179
A team of researchers from the University
of Utah announced they had found

00:01:26.179 --> 00:01:27.350
the gene—BRCA1.

00:01:27.350 --> 00:01:32.150
Soon after, they found another similar gene
with nearly identical affects

00:01:32.150 --> 00:01:33.969
—BRCA2.

00:01:33.969 --> 00:01:38.330
Females found to have a mutation in one of
these two genes have an 80%

00:01:38.330 --> 00:01:42.380
chance of contracting breast cancer in their
lifetimes, but the good news was that you

00:01:42.380 --> 00:01:43.670
could
test for it.

00:01:43.670 --> 00:01:47.719
This was a life-changing breakthrough for
the thousands found to have the mutation

00:01:47.719 --> 00:01:49.079
yearly.

00:01:49.079 --> 00:01:52.851
Some of the researchers from the University
of Utah saw the commercial application

00:01:52.851 --> 00:01:57.899
of this and founded a company—Myriad Genetics—which
soon patented the two genes,

00:01:57.899 --> 00:01:58.969
quite literally.

00:01:58.969 --> 00:02:04.710
In their patent document is the full genetic
sequence of the BRCA1 gene.

00:02:04.710 --> 00:02:08.840
21,328
characters of G’s, C’s, A’s, and T’s

00:02:08.840 --> 00:02:11.640
that represent what is in the human body.

00:02:11.640 --> 00:02:15.239
They patented
something inside you, something not all that

00:02:15.239 --> 00:02:17.580
different than patenting that apple.

00:02:17.580 --> 00:02:21.640
The apple is too
just a long sequence of G’s, C’s, A’s,

00:02:21.640 --> 00:02:22.640
and T’s.

00:02:22.640 --> 00:02:24.810
The only difference is that it’s longer.

00:02:24.810 --> 00:02:27.959
Myriad
genetics, as the only company globally allowed

00:02:27.959 --> 00:02:33.129
to do anything with the BRCA genes, had an
absolute monopoly on testing.

00:02:33.129 --> 00:02:35.760
If you wanted to see if you had the gene mutation
that would

00:02:35.760 --> 00:02:41.709
give you a heads up on cancer, you had to
go to Myriad, and so it came at a price—up

00:02:41.709 --> 00:02:45.520
to
$4,000 even though the actual process of performing

00:02:45.520 --> 00:02:48.330
the test cost only a fraction of that.

00:02:48.330 --> 00:02:52.720
This was almost certainly unjust, and so the
ACLU took notice.

00:02:52.720 --> 00:02:56.220
They, along with a
group of plaintiffs, sued Myriad Genetics

00:02:56.220 --> 00:03:01.870
on the basis that their patents were on non-patentable
material—the human genome.

00:03:01.870 --> 00:03:04.519
Much like you can’t patent an apple because
you didn’t create

00:03:04.519 --> 00:03:09.190
the apple, the plaintiffs argued that Myriad
couldn’t patent the genes because they didn’t

00:03:09.190 --> 00:03:14.220
create them, nature did, but Myriad was not
alone in the process of patenting genes.

00:03:14.220 --> 00:03:18.069
In
fact, at the time, 21 percent of the human

00:03:18.069 --> 00:03:19.540
genome had been patented.

00:03:19.540 --> 00:03:22.520
The District Court
ended up ruling in the plaintiffs favor, so

00:03:22.520 --> 00:03:27.239
Myriad appealed and the case went to a Federal
Appeals court that ended up ruling in Myriad’s

00:03:27.239 --> 00:03:29.750
favor so the ACLU petitioned for their case
to be

00:03:29.750 --> 00:03:31.590
heard in the Supreme Court.

00:03:31.590 --> 00:03:35.280
They agreed, and on April 15th, 2013 the case
of the Association for

00:03:35.280 --> 00:03:39.900
Molecular Pathology vs Myriad Genetics, Inc
reached Washington.

00:03:39.900 --> 00:03:44.620
In a stunning turn of events,
all nine Supreme Court justices sided in the

00:03:44.620 --> 00:03:47.060
the plaintiffs favor therefore virtually ending
the

00:03:47.060 --> 00:03:51.379
process of human genome patents, but was it
right?

00:03:51.379 --> 00:03:56.090
Up to this point Myriad has probably seemed
like the bad guy, but consider this.

00:03:56.090 --> 00:03:58.420
The
individuals who founded Myriad were the ones

00:03:58.420 --> 00:04:00.790
that discovered the effect of the gene mutation
in

00:04:00.790 --> 00:04:04.450
question, so shouldn’t they be rewarded
for their years of work?

00:04:04.450 --> 00:04:07.049
That’s really the question with
all patents.

00:04:07.049 --> 00:04:11.519
On one hand patents drive innovation because
they allow companies to monetize

00:04:11.519 --> 00:04:17.040
discoveries that cost huge amounts to research
and develop, but on the other hand restrictive

00:04:17.040 --> 00:04:22.910
patents and monopolies stop these innovations
from getting to the people who need them most.

00:04:22.910 --> 00:04:27.750
Without the ability to make money off of genetic
testing, it can be argued that many companies

00:04:27.750 --> 00:04:31.160
won’t have the motivation to research genetic
predispositions.

00:04:31.160 --> 00:04:35.610
So that brings us to the critical
question: what should be patentable?

00:04:35.610 --> 00:04:39.330
There are certainly cases where patents on
genetic material have helped.

00:04:39.330 --> 00:04:42.200
In this age
where editing genome sequences is cheap and

00:04:42.200 --> 00:04:44.940
easy, it’s very possible for companies to
modify

00:04:44.940 --> 00:04:50.680
organisms in a way that make them true, original
creations that can be legitimately patented.

00:04:50.680 --> 00:04:52.850
For
example, the apple we talked about at the

00:04:52.850 --> 00:04:57.770
beginning could not be patented, but a
genetically modified apple—that’s fair

00:04:57.770 --> 00:04:58.770
game.

00:04:58.770 --> 00:05:02.810
In fact, patents on genetically modified food
have existed for decades.

00:05:02.810 --> 00:05:06.540
Golden Rice is a much lauded yet controversial
variety of genetically

00:05:06.540 --> 00:05:11.640
modified rice that includes a high amount
of beta-carotene which helps treat vitamin-a

00:05:11.640 --> 00:05:14.820
deficiency
—an affliction that kills up to two million

00:05:14.820 --> 00:05:17.740
individuals yearly in developing countries.

00:05:17.740 --> 00:05:20.810
As a crop
that is cheap, easy to cultivate, and already

00:05:20.810 --> 00:05:24.130
popular in developing countries, a fortified
variety of

00:05:24.130 --> 00:05:26.300
rice can help millions yearly.

00:05:26.300 --> 00:05:29.890
The rice does not, however, have much of a
commercial application

00:05:29.890 --> 00:05:34.090
since the places that need it are the places
without the money to buy other treatments

00:05:34.090 --> 00:05:36.190
to vitamin a
deficiency.

00:05:36.190 --> 00:05:40.230
Nonetheless, by holding the patent, the owners
are able to manage the distribution

00:05:40.230 --> 00:05:46.180
of the seeds by giving them for free to any
farmer who makes less than $10,000 a year

00:05:46.180 --> 00:05:48.510
while
funding the program with limited commercial

00:05:48.510 --> 00:05:50.150
applications elsewhere.

00:05:50.150 --> 00:05:53.860
Although, some companies have a more commercial
focus.

00:05:53.860 --> 00:05:58.570
93% of corn in the US is
genetically modified and much of that comes

00:05:58.570 --> 00:06:00.040
from Monsanto.

00:06:00.040 --> 00:06:03.210
Monsanto sells different
varieties of genetically modified corn and

00:06:03.210 --> 00:06:05.550
other crops under different brands just like
you

00:06:05.550 --> 00:06:07.150
would any other product.

00:06:07.150 --> 00:06:10.240
There’s SmartStax Rib complete which is
less susceptible to

00:06:10.240 --> 00:06:14.820
destructive insects; there’s Roundup Ready
corn that isn't affected by Roundup, a

00:06:14.820 --> 00:06:18.540
herbicide that kills weeds; and there’s
DroughtGard corn which is more tolerant to

00:06:18.540 --> 00:06:20.960
lower
levels of water during dry years.

00:06:20.960 --> 00:06:24.210
When a farmer wants to use one of these variants
of corn

00:06:24.210 --> 00:06:28.600
they just go to a dealer and buy the seeds
like any other product, except the difference

00:06:28.600 --> 00:06:31.410
between
seeds and traditional products is that seeds

00:06:31.410 --> 00:06:34.510
grow into plants which make more seeds.

00:06:34.510 --> 00:06:37.840
If Monsanto
allowed farmers to keep and share their seeds

00:06:37.840 --> 00:06:40.650
they would only be able to sell the product
once,

00:06:40.650 --> 00:06:41.980
so they don’t.

00:06:41.980 --> 00:06:46.200
Those who buy the product are required to
sign an agreement to not save or share

00:06:46.200 --> 00:06:51.110
any seed and those who do and get caught are
met by a hefty lawsuit.

00:06:51.110 --> 00:06:54.020
But this isn't really
different than any other product.

00:06:54.020 --> 00:06:56.600
This is their form of rights management.

00:06:56.600 --> 00:06:59.591
When you watch a
video on Netflix, in the terms of use, you

00:06:59.591 --> 00:07:03.890
have agreed to only use their product for
yourself just like the farmers agreed to only

00:07:03.890 --> 00:07:08.720
use the Monsanto seed for themselves even
though, with both products, it’s rather

00:07:08.720 --> 00:07:10.250
easy to share.

00:07:10.250 --> 00:07:14.000
Just like with patented gene sequences,
there’s the question of where the line is

00:07:14.000 --> 00:07:17.400
between patents improving and harming the
greater good.

00:07:17.400 --> 00:07:21.300
If companies are not able to recuperate research
and development cost, they

00:07:21.300 --> 00:07:23.060
won’t research or develop.

00:07:23.060 --> 00:07:26.190
In a free market, farmers can choose whether
or not to buy the

00:07:26.190 --> 00:07:31.860
Monsanto seeds given the terms and, overwhelmingly,
they do because the seeds make more

00:07:31.860 --> 00:07:33.290
money than they cost.

00:07:33.290 --> 00:07:35.240
It’s just simple math.

00:07:35.240 --> 00:07:39.480
You should form your own opinion on patented
genetically modified plants and seeds,

00:07:39.480 --> 00:07:45.220
but it’s indisputable that there are upsides
that can be argued, though, what about animals?

00:07:45.220 --> 00:07:48.450
In
1980 the US supreme court decided that genetically

00:07:48.450 --> 00:07:52.680
modified animals could be patented if they
were altered in a meaningful way from their

00:07:52.680 --> 00:07:54.030
original selves.

00:07:54.030 --> 00:07:56.380
European patent law considers the
same true.

00:07:56.380 --> 00:08:00.920
In fact, you might already be eating genetically
modified animals without even

00:08:00.920 --> 00:08:02.060
knowing it.

00:08:02.060 --> 00:08:05.840
The company AquaBounty Technologies successfully
created a variant16 of

00:08:05.840 --> 00:08:11.190
salmon that grows at a faster rate and so
they patented it—the AquAdvantage salmon—and

00:08:11.190 --> 00:08:14.900
in
2016, it hit the shelves in Canada.

00:08:14.900 --> 00:08:18.000
Researchers have found the salmon safe to
eat, but it is, of

00:08:18.000 --> 00:08:19.590
course, different.

00:08:19.590 --> 00:08:23.100
If the genetically modified salmon were to
be accidentally released into the

00:08:23.100 --> 00:08:27.640
wild, some are concerned that their genetic
makeup could spread by outcompeting the normal

00:08:27.640 --> 00:08:32.519
salmon, although in computer models it was
concluded that the normal salmon would prevail

00:08:32.519 --> 00:08:35.029
since it has a higher fertility rate.

00:08:35.029 --> 00:08:37.969
In the US, the AquAdvantage salmon has been
approved by the

00:08:37.969 --> 00:08:43.709
FDA, but it cannot be sold until labeling
standards for genetically food are developed.

00:08:43.709 --> 00:08:46.269
But just imagine the next step.

00:08:46.269 --> 00:08:48.860
Genetically modified humans are coming and
whether or

00:08:48.860 --> 00:08:53.310
not companies will be allowed to patent their
processes will have a huge effect on how

00:08:53.310 --> 00:08:56.639
commonplace human gene editing will become.

00:08:56.639 --> 00:09:01.330
The Myriad genetics case only decided that
patents on naturally occurring gene sequences

00:09:01.330 --> 00:09:06.600
were not allowed, but the decision that allowed
patents on genetically modified animals specifically

00:09:06.600 --> 00:09:11.970
excluded humans meaning that as of now
you would not be granted a patent for human

00:09:11.970 --> 00:09:14.970
gene editing in the US, but there are plenty
of other

00:09:14.970 --> 00:09:16.250
countries.

00:09:16.250 --> 00:09:19.030
Most have not even considered this question.

00:09:19.030 --> 00:09:23.829
If you imagine a world with patented,
commercial human gene editing, you are imaging

00:09:23.829 --> 00:09:28.560
a world where you can decide if your child
will have blue eyes or not, if they’re tall

00:09:28.560 --> 00:09:33.600
or not, if they’re smart or not, if they
are autistic or not,

00:09:33.600 --> 00:09:39.250
if they will have heart disease or not, if
they will have alzheimer’s or not, if they

00:09:39.250 --> 00:09:41.480
will have cancer
or not.

00:09:41.480 --> 00:09:45.950
The real question is not if it’s ethical
to allow patented human gene editing.

00:09:45.950 --> 00:09:49.579
It’s if it’s
ethical to not.

00:09:49.579 --> 00:09:52.862
If you want to learn more about genetic research,
there’s a great course that I

00:09:52.862 --> 00:09:57.129
recommend on Skillshare called “An Introduction
To Bioinformatics.”

00:09:57.129 --> 00:10:00.230
As you’ve heard
